WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:03.796
In part one

2
00:00:03.871 --> 00:00:06.559
every step of elimination subtracted

3
00:00:06.633 --> 00:00:09.247
a multiple of one row from another.

4
00:00:09.247 --> 00:00:12.084
Take the small system from the check

5
00:00:12.084 --> 00:00:14.249
three x plus y equals seven

6
00:00:14.249 --> 00:00:17.460
and six x plus five y equals twenty three.

7
00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:20.589
Its matrix has two columns.

8
00:00:20.589 --> 00:00:22.750
Draw them as arrows.

9
00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:25.426
Column one is three, six

10
00:00:25.426 --> 00:00:28.410
and column two is one, five.

11
00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:32.597
Elimination wants a zero below the pivot

12
00:00:32.597 --> 00:00:33.252
three.

13
00:00:33.252 --> 00:00:36.902
So where must the first column end up?

14
00:00:36.902 --> 00:00:38.680
Pause and predict.

15
00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:45.341
It must land on the horizontal axis

16
00:00:45.341 --> 00:00:48.080
where the second entry is zero.

17
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.932
The step is row two minus two times row one.

18
00:00:51.932 --> 00:00:55.270
As a matrix, that is the identity with

19
00:00:55.355 --> 00:00:58.180
minus two in row two, column one.

20
00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.994
Now watch the whole plane.

21
00:01:00.994 --> 00:01:04.311
Every point keeps its first entry

22
00:01:04.311 --> 00:01:07.912
and loses twice that from its second.

23
00:01:07.912 --> 00:01:10.850
The grid shears straight down.

24
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.536
Column one, three, six, drops to three

25
00:01:14.536 --> 00:01:16.845
zero, right onto the axis.

26
00:01:16.845 --> 00:01:20.608
Column two, one, five, drops to one, three.

27
00:01:20.608 --> 00:01:24.200
Those are the columns of the staircase U.

28
00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.233
The right side takes the same step

29
00:01:28.233 --> 00:01:29.516
seven stays

30
00:01:29.516 --> 00:01:33.561
and twenty three minus fourteen is nine.

31
00:01:33.561 --> 00:01:38.001
So the bottom row reads three y equals nine

32
00:01:38.001 --> 00:01:39.580
and y is three.

33
00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:43.935
Then three x plus three equals seven

34
00:01:43.935 --> 00:01:46.209
so x is four thirds.

35
00:01:46.317 --> 00:01:49.348
One step on the rows of A is

36
00:01:49.456 --> 00:01:51.946
one matrix on its left

37
00:01:51.946 --> 00:01:55.410
and in the plane it is a shear.

38
00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:59.133
Back to the paint matrix, three by three.

39
00:01:59.133 --> 00:02:01.815
Its first step has the same shape

40
00:02:01.815 --> 00:02:04.881
the identity with minus two in row two

41
00:02:04.881 --> 00:02:05.801
column one.

42
00:02:05.801 --> 00:02:07.488
We call it E two one

43
00:02:07.488 --> 00:02:10.630
because it removes the entry in row two

44
00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:11.550
column one.

45
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.937
Multiply E two one times A.

46
00:02:13.937 --> 00:02:17.181
Rows one and three come through unchanged.

47
00:02:17.181 --> 00:02:18.841
Only row two changes

48
00:02:18.841 --> 00:02:21.180
and it now starts with a zero.

49
00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:22.470
Why rows?

50
00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:26.649
Row two of E two one is minus two, one

51
00:02:26.649 --> 00:02:27.276
zero.

52
00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:30.932
So row two of the product is minus

53
00:02:31.037 --> 00:02:33.439
two times row one of A

54
00:02:33.439 --> 00:02:35.947
plus one times row two.

55
00:02:36.051 --> 00:02:38.663
A matrix on the left of A

56
00:02:38.767 --> 00:02:41.170
combines the rows of A.

57
00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:45.294
The other two steps are matrices too.

58
00:02:45.294 --> 00:02:49.125
E three one has minus one in row three

59
00:02:49.125 --> 00:02:52.765
column one, and E three two has minus

60
00:02:52.861 --> 00:02:55.830
three in row three, column two.

61
00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:58.316
Multiply them in order

62
00:02:58.409 --> 00:03:00.732
E three two times E three

63
00:03:00.824 --> 00:03:02.682
one times E two one

64
00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:05.097
and you get one matrix E.

65
00:03:05.097 --> 00:03:07.884
E times A is the staircase U

66
00:03:07.884 --> 00:03:11.878
and E times b is the new right side: five

67
00:03:11.878 --> 00:03:12.900
five, two.

68
00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:17.000
Look at the bottom left corner of E: a five.

69
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.495
The multipliers were two, one and three

70
00:03:20.495 --> 00:03:22.200
yet a five appears.

71
00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.950
The single matrix mixes the steps together

72
00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:29.530
lecture four keeps the multipliers clean.

73
00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:31.394
Order matters.

74
00:03:31.394 --> 00:03:35.247
Put the step matrix on the right instead

75
00:03:35.247 --> 00:03:36.990
A times E two one.

76
00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.376
This product has no new zero at all.

77
00:03:40.376 --> 00:03:43.749
A matrix on the right combines columns

78
00:03:43.749 --> 00:03:44.592
not rows.

79
00:03:44.677 --> 00:03:47.122
So an elimination step always

80
00:03:47.207 --> 00:03:50.917
goes on the left, and in a product of steps

81
00:03:50.917 --> 00:03:53.700
the one on the right acts first.

82
00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:57.466
Elimination divides by every pivot.

83
00:03:57.466 --> 00:04:00.015
So what if a pivot is zero?

84
00:04:00.015 --> 00:04:01.836
Take this matrix B.

85
00:04:01.836 --> 00:04:03.839
After the first step

86
00:04:03.839 --> 00:04:07.480
the second pivot position holds a zero.

87
00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:11.238
But row three has a one in that column.

88
00:04:11.238 --> 00:04:13.772
Exchange rows two and three.

89
00:04:13.772 --> 00:04:16.482
That exchange is a matrix too

90
00:04:16.482 --> 00:04:18.404
a permutation matrix

91
00:04:18.404 --> 00:04:21.550
the identity with two rows swapped.

92
00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:25.258
Now the pivots are one, one and three.

93
00:04:25.258 --> 00:04:28.174
The zero only meant a bad order.

94
00:04:28.174 --> 00:04:31.356
B is fine, and every right side has

95
00:04:31.444 --> 00:04:33.300
exactly one solution.

96
00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:35.847
Now change one entry

97
00:04:35.847 --> 00:04:39.688
the seven in the last row becomes a six.

98
00:04:39.688 --> 00:04:41.843
Call the new matrix C

99
00:04:41.843 --> 00:04:44.560
and do the first step again.

100
00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:48.695
Both rows below have a zero in column two.

101
00:04:48.695 --> 00:04:51.488
Can a row exchange still help?

102
00:04:51.488 --> 00:04:53.110
Pause and decide.

103
00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:57.019
No.

104
00:04:57.019 --> 00:04:59.156
Swapping a zero for a zero

105
00:04:59.238 --> 00:05:00.882
still leaves a zero

106
00:05:00.882 --> 00:05:03.265
so there is no second pivot.

107
00:05:03.265 --> 00:05:04.991
Look at the columns

108
00:05:04.991 --> 00:05:08.690
column two of C is exactly twice column one.

109
00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:10.613
In the paint story

110
00:05:10.696 --> 00:05:13.181
one paint is a double strength

111
00:05:13.263 --> 00:05:14.588
copy of another

112
00:05:14.588 --> 00:05:17.487
and no recipe can tell them apart.

113
00:05:17.487 --> 00:05:20.799
C is singular: fewer than three pivots

114
00:05:20.799 --> 00:05:24.360
and either no solution or infinitely many.

115
00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:26.986
For a big system you will

116
00:05:27.068 --> 00:05:28.705
not do this by hand.

117
00:05:28.705 --> 00:05:31.815
numpy's solve calls a LAPACK routine

118
00:05:31.897 --> 00:05:34.434
and that routine is elimination

119
00:05:34.516 --> 00:05:36.071
with row exchanges.

120
00:05:36.071 --> 00:05:39.509
For the paint system it returns one, one

121
00:05:39.509 --> 00:05:40.900
two, our answer.

122
00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:44.661
It records the steps as a factorization

123
00:05:44.661 --> 00:05:48.498
A equals P L U, the subject of lecture four.

124
00:05:48.584 --> 00:05:50.545
And when a library says

125
00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:52.421
a matrix is singular

126
00:05:52.421 --> 00:05:54.723
it means what we just saw

127
00:05:54.723 --> 00:05:56.770
a column with no pivot.

128
00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:00.668
Last, a habit for working with AI.

129
00:06:00.668 --> 00:06:03.498
Suppose an assistant writes

130
00:06:03.498 --> 00:06:07.694
row two plus two times row one gives zero

131
00:06:07.694 --> 00:06:08.670
one, two.

132
00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:12.613
Build the matrix it named, and multiply.

133
00:06:12.613 --> 00:06:16.115
Row two comes out as eight, five, six

134
00:06:16.115 --> 00:06:17.911
not zero, one, two.

135
00:06:17.911 --> 00:06:19.708
The sign was wrong

136
00:06:19.708 --> 00:06:23.300
the row it showed belongs to minus two.

137
00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:25.490
Every step is a matrix

138
00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:27.810
so every step can be checked

139
00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:29.810
with one multiplication.

140
00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:31.321
To sum up.

141
00:06:31.414 --> 00:06:35.136
An elimination step is the identity with

142
00:06:35.229 --> 00:06:38.672
minus the multiplier in one position

143
00:06:38.672 --> 00:06:40.905
multiplied on the left.

144
00:06:40.905 --> 00:06:43.790
On the columns, it is a shear.

145
00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:47.661
The steps combine into one matrix E

146
00:06:47.661 --> 00:06:49.715
with E A equal to U.

147
00:06:49.813 --> 00:06:52.552
And a zero pivot is repaired

148
00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:54.410
by a row exchange

149
00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:57.540
or else the matrix is singular.

150
00:06:57.890 --> 00:06:59.055
Now you try

151
00:06:59.152 --> 00:07:02.646
write the matrix that subtracts four

152
00:07:02.743 --> 00:07:05.558
times row one from row three

153
00:07:05.558 --> 00:07:09.149
and check it by computing E times A.

154
00:07:09.149 --> 00:07:10.508
Next lecture

155
00:07:10.508 --> 00:07:13.905
multiplying matrices in four ways

156
00:07:13.905 --> 00:07:17.690
and undoing a matrix with its inverse.
